---
.title = "Object-capability RPC for OCapN, in Zig",
.description = "glowcap is a Zig library for OCapN and CapTP: object-capability RPC with no ambient authority to fall back on.",
.date = .date("2026-06-12T00:00:00"),
.layout = "index.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

glowcap is a [Zig](https://ziglang.org/) library for
[OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn). Two programs hand each other
references to objects and call them across a session, under the same rule as a
local call: a peer can use the references it was given and cannot forge others.

glowcap is built with AI coding assistants. The maintainer directs the work
and is answerable for what lands.
[PROVENANCE.md](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/PROVENANCE.md)
records how.

If capabilities are new to you, [Why OCapN]($link.page('why')) compares them
with the bearer tokens and login tables you already use.

[Their program hands yours a reference to an object called "greeter." Holding that reference is the whole permission to call it.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-capability.svg'))

[Rooms]($link.page('rooms')) is a chat app built on this idea, where the
peers are people: open a room, pick a member, and press Message. Native peers
connect over [iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')), which is encrypted QUIC
that uses the node id as the peer's identity.

A native Zig fetch-and-call over sealed TCP:

```zig
var hint_storage: [1]glowcap.ocapn.model.HintEntry = undefined;
const peer_key = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>";
const partner = glowcap.net.tcp.noise.locator(
    peer_key,
    "127.0.0.1:9099",
    &hint_storage,
);
const session = try glowcap.driver.connect(
    allocator,
    io,
    .{ .symbols = &.{.noise_tcp} },
    partner,
    .ephemeral,
    .{ .handshake = .{ .ms = 10_000 }, .settle = .{ .ms = 10_000 } },
);
defer session.deinit();
const greeter = try session.fetch("greeter"); // a capability this peer published
var arena = std.heap.ArenaAllocator.init(allocator);
defer arena.deinit();
const settled = try session.call(greeter, "greet", &.{.{ .string = "world" }}, arena.allocator());
```

That is the whole exchange: dial the peer, fetch a capability it published,
and call it. [Start building]($link.page('start')) fills in the other languages and
transports.

Pin a commit, and expect the API to move.
[What you can depend on]($link.page('stability')) separates checked behavior
from areas that still need external evidence.

The protocol core does no I/O of its own. It is a synchronous state machine you
feed bytes into and drain bytes out of, so sockets, threads, and scheduling stay
in your program where you can see them. Peers can meet over encrypted discovery
([iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh'))), sealed TCP
([Noise]($link.page('transports/noise'))), a browser DataChannel
([WebRTC]($link.page('transports/webrtc'))), or Tor
([onion]($link.page('transports/onion'))), among other paths.
[Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing')) is the decision table.

Objects can also outlive their process. [Loam]($link.page('loam')), the
storage layer, keeps objects and the capabilities they hold on disk. An actor
nobody is calling can sleep there, and a peer's sturdyref still reaches it
after a restart. Loam's full API is in Zig; C and Rust get a narrower
profile, and Rust's also runs in the browser on IndexedDB.

Use the library as a Zig package, a plain C ABI, or a native binding (Rust,
TypeScript, Python, Racket). The TypeScript binding also compiles to
WebAssembly, so the same session runs in the browser. The [CapTP
CLI]($link.page('cli')) exposes the same operations from a shell; `zig build`
puts the `glowcap` binary in `zig-out/bin`. [Choose an
API]($link.page('surfaces')) compares all of these entry points.

Ready to dial a peer? Go to [Start building]($link.page('start')).
